  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="cover">
      <div class="company">SALE FISH MARKETING AND CONSULTING</div>
      <div class="tagline">OT Cybersecurity Practice</div>
      <div class="title">Multi-Site Portfolio<br/>Rollup Report</div>
      <p style="margin-top:30pt; font-size:12pt;">3 Sites</p>
      <p style="font-size:11pt; color:#595959;">November 21, 2026</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Executive Summary</h1>
    <div style="margin-top:14pt;">
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">3</div><div class="lbl">Sites Included</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">2.6</div><div class="lbl">Combined Maturity (avg)</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">51</div><div class="lbl">Total Risks (all sites)</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>This report rolls up 3 individual site assessments into a combined portfolio view. The lowest-scoring site is <strong>Riverside Production Facility</strong> at 1.9/5.0. Combined average maturity across all included sites is <strong>2.6/5.0</strong>.</p>

    <h2>Maturity by Site</h2>
    <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="600" style="width:600px;" />
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>Site</th><th>Industry</th><th>Standard</th><th>Overall Maturity</th><th>CMM Level</th><th>Risks</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Riverside Production Facility</td><td>Chemical</td><td>IEC 62443</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1.9</td>
      <td>Initial</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">20</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Bayport Distribution Terminal</td><td>Chemical</td><td>NIST SP 800-82r3</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.9</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">20</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Lake Charles Pipeline Station</td><td>Oil &amp; Gas</td><td>TSA Pipeline Security Directives</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.9</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">11</td>
    </tr></table>

    
      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h2>Riverside Production Facility</h2>
      <table class="meta-table">
        <tr><td class="meta-label">Organization:</td><td>Meridian Chemical Solutions</td><td class="meta-label">Industry:</td><td>Chemical</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="meta-label">Standard:</td><td>IEC 62443</td><td class="meta-label">Overall Maturity:</td><td>1.9</td></tr>
      </table>
      <h3>Top Risks at This Site</h3>
      <ul><li>FR3 — System Integrity: Communication integrity is protected on conduits carrying safety-relevant or high-consequence traffic. (score 1/5) — Implement control-logic change detection tied to your Management of Change process. Verify firmware/software integrity via cryptographic signature before deployment, and protect communication integrity on safety-relevant conduits.</li><li>FR5 — Restricted Data Flow: Zone/conduit design is re-validated when new device classes are introduced (e.g., IIoT gateways, wireless). (score 1/5) — Partition OT into risk-based zones (not just topology-convenient ones). Enforce default-deny conduit allow-lists between every zone, and re-validate zone/conduit design whenever new device classes (e.g., IIoT gateways) are introduced.</li><li>FR2 — Use Control: Use of portable media and mobile code is restricted and controlled at OT boundaries. (score 1/5) — Enforce least-privilege authorization consistently across zones, scaled to each zone's Security Level. Apply session timeout/concurrent-session limits at jump hosts, and restrict portable media and mobile code at OT boundaries.</li><li>FR6 — Timely Response to Events: Every zone conduit has an associated detection sensor (passive network monitoring). (score 1/5) — Ensure every zone conduit has an associated passive detection sensor. Map detection use cases to known adversary tactics/techniques (MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), and rehearse the incident response process via tabletop exercise at least annually.</li><li>FR6 — Timely Response to Events: Detection use cases are mapped to known adversary tactics/techniques (e.g., MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), not just generic anomaly rules. (score 1/5) — Ensure every zone conduit has an associated passive detection sensor. Map detection use cases to known adversary tactics/techniques (MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), and rehearse the incident response process via tabletop exercise at least annually.</li></ul>
    
      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h2>Bayport Distribution Terminal</h2>
      <table class="meta-table">
        <tr><td class="meta-label">Organization:</td><td>Meridian Chemical Solutions</td><td class="meta-label">Industry:</td><td>Chemical</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="meta-label">Standard:</td><td>NIST SP 800-82r3</td><td class="meta-label">Overall Maturity:</td><td>2.9</td></tr>
      </table>
      <h3>Top Risks at This Site</h3>
      <ul><li>Incident Response: Regulatory incident-reporting obligations (e.g., CISA, sector-specific) are built into the IR process as an explicit step. (score 2/5) — Document and rehearse a safety-first containment decision process (joint IR/operations/safety decision for SIS-adjacent assets). Build regulatory reporting timelines into the IR process explicitly, not as an afterthought.</li><li>System &amp; Information Integrity: Firmware updates are verified against vendor cryptographic signatures before deployment. (score 2/5) — Deploy control-logic and configuration integrity monitoring, especially on engineering workstations. Verify firmware updates against vendor cryptographic signatures before deployment. Document compensating controls for legacy devices that cannot run endpoint protection.</li><li>Contingency Planning: Restore capability is actually tested on a defined cadence, not merely assumed to work. (score 2/5) — Establish verified 'golden image' backups (logic + configuration + firmware) for high-criticality assets, refreshed on every approved change. Actually test restore capability on a defined cadence, and define RTO/RPO jointly with operations/safety.</li><li>Identification &amp; Authentication: Device and software identity (certificates, keys) is managed with a defined lifecycle (issuance, rotation, revocation). (score 2/5) — Enforce MFA on all remote and administrative OT access. Establish a certificate/key lifecycle process (issuance, rotation, revocation) for device and software identity, particularly for OPC-UA and CIP Security deployments.</li><li>Configuration Management: Patches are validated in a representative staging environment before deployment to production Tier 1/2 assets. (score 2/5) — Implement a formal Management of Change (MOC) process with risk, safety, and security review gates for every change to high-criticality assets. Maintain a current asset inventory reconciled at least annually, and validate patches in a staging environment before production deployment.</li></ul>
    
      <div class="page-break"></div>
      <h2>Lake Charles Pipeline Station</h2>
      <table class="meta-table">
        <tr><td class="meta-label">Organization:</td><td>Meridian Midstream LLC</td><td class="meta-label">Industry:</td><td>Oil &amp; Gas</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="meta-label">Standard:</td><td>TSA Pipeline Security Directives</td><td class="meta-label">Overall Maturity:</td><td>2.9</td></tr>
      </table>
      <h3>Top Risks at This Site</h3>
      <ul><li>Incident Reporting &amp; Response: Incident response roles and escalation paths are defined and rehearsed, not only documented on paper. (score 2/5) — Document and rehearse a process ensuring qualifying incidents are reported to CISA within the required timeframe. Define and rehearse incident response roles and escalation paths.</li><li>Patch &amp; Vulnerability Management: Compensating controls are documented and applied for vulnerabilities that cannot be immediately patched. (score 2/5) — Document a risk-based patch management process (deliberate, tracked, not necessarily immediate). Maintain documented compensating controls for vulnerabilities that cannot be immediately patched.</li><li>Cybersecurity Assessment Program: The effectiveness of the measures in the Cybersecurity Implementation Plan is assessed on the required cadence, including an architectural design review, by assessors independent of the team implementing them. (score 2/5) — Run the TSA-required Cybersecurity Assessment Program on its required cadence, including the architectural design review, with assessors independent of the team implementing the controls and findings tracked to closure with assigned ownership and timelines.</li><li>Cybersecurity Assessment Program: Assessment findings are tracked to closure with assigned ownership and timelines. (score 2/5) — Run the TSA-required Cybersecurity Assessment Program on its required cadence, including the architectural design review, with assessors independent of the team implementing the controls and findings tracked to closure with assigned ownership and timelines.</li><li>IT/OT Network Segmentation: Segmentation architecture is documented and available for TSA/CISA review upon request. (score 3/5) — Document a formal industrial DMZ / segmentation architecture separating IT and OT, and keep the documentation current for TSA/CISA review.</li></ul>
    

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Methodology Note</h1>
    <p>Each site was assessed independently using the tool's standard question bank; this report aggregates those independent results. Sites using different standards or assessment modes (short/long) are shown side-by-side for comparison but their underlying question sets differ — treat cross-site comparisons as directional, not a precise apples-to-apples score.</p>
    <p class="note">Report generated by the Sale Fish Marketing and Consulting OT Cybersecurity Assessment Tool — Question Bank v2.1.0 (2026-11-21).</p>
  </body></html>